// What an assertion's `values:` rows say when the subject is an IMPL CALL.
//
// The assertions below FAIL ON PURPOSE. That is not a stylistic choice, it is
// the only way this surface is observable at all: a PASSING assertion renders
// no operand, and every assertion in the corpus passes, which is exactly why
// `directImplCall records no argument rows` sat unnoticed. The whole
// differential sweep is blind to it.
//
// # What differed at the parent
//
// Every other call path routes its arguments through `recordCallArgs` —
// native.go's user-function path, the stdlib path, lists/maps/sets/ranges,
// the prelude paths, the channel and concurrency paths. `directImplCallAt`
// did not, so a failing assertion whose subject reached an impl produced a
// report with the CALL's row and none of its ARGUMENTS', while the
// interpreter (eval's recordAssertionCallSlots) produced both.
//
// # The four spellings, and why all four are here
//
// One route (`directImplCallAt`) is reached by four different syntaxes, and a
// fix placed at any one call site rather than inside the shared function would
// serve some and not others. Each row below enters through a different door:
//
//	Counter.bump(c, 3)          type-qualified          typeQualifiedCall
//	Bumper.bump(c, 3)           interface-qualified     interfaceCall
//	bump(c, 3)                  bare, same owner        implSelfCall / barecall
//	Boxer.wrap(b, 2)            generic impl template   genericImplCall
//
// # The LITERAL rule, which is the part a careless fix gets wrong
//
// `recordCallArgs` includes literal arguments only when the callee returns
// Bool — a predicate's literal inputs are what the reader wants (`assert
// even?(5)` shows `5 = 5`), an ordinary call's are noise (`assert double(3) ==
// 7` must not print `3 = 3`). The interpreter decides that from the call's
// runtime result and the builder from the callee's declared return type, so
// the two agree only if the builder reads the SELECTED implementation's
// result. `bump` returns Int and `even_sum?` returns Bool, so the `3` is
// dropped in one report and printed in the other, a few lines apart.
interface Bumper {
    fn bump(c: self, by: Int): Int
    fn even_sum?(c: self, by: Int): Bool
}

struct Counter {
    n: Int
}

impl Bumper for Counter {
    fn bump(c: Counter, by: Int): Int {
        c.n + by
    }

    fn even_sum?(c: Counter, by: Int): Bool {
        (c.n + by) % 2 == 0
    }
}

interface Wrapper {
    fn wrap(b: self, by: Int): Int
}

struct Boxer<T> {
    item: T
    seed: Int
}

impl Wrapper for Boxer<T> {
    fn wrap(b: Boxer<T>, by: Int): Int {
        b.seed * by
    }
}

// A NON-LITERAL second argument, so the row appears under the Int-returning
// callee too — where the literal rule would have dropped a literal. Without
// this the Int rows could only ever show the receiver, and a fix that recorded
// the receiver alone would look complete.
fn step(): Int {
    2
}

test "a type-qualified impl call records its arguments" {
    c = Counter{n: 5}
    assert Counter.bump(c, step()) == 99
}

test "an interface-qualified impl call records its arguments" {
    c = Counter{n: 5}
    assert Bumper.bump(c, step()) == 99
}

test "a Bool-returning impl call records its LITERAL arguments too" {
    c = Counter{n: 5}
    assert Bumper.even_sum?(c, 2)
}

test "an Int-returning impl call drops its literal argument" {
    c = Counter{n: 5}
    assert Counter.bump(c, 3) == 99
}

test "a generic impl template's call records its arguments" {
    b = Boxer{item: "x", seed: 4}
    assert Boxer.wrap(b, step()) == 99
}

// The OPERATOR interface written out as a CALL. It reaches
// `directImplCallAt` through operCall / operTypeQualifiedCall, which are the
// fifth and sixth doors, and it is here because the argument-row recording is
// gated on the node being an `*ast.Call`: the `a + b` spelling arrives as an
// `*ast.Binary`, has no argument list, and records through binary()'s own
// `recordComparisonOperands`, so these two spellings are the only operator
// routes the recording can reach.
//
// A SINGLE unambiguous impl on purpose. A second `impl Add<Days, X> for Day`
// differing only in the OUTPUT type analyses clean and then panics at load
// ("impl-dispatch collision: Add.add for Day\x00Days registered twice"), which
// is a separate open defect and not this fixture's subject.
type Day Int

type Days Int

fn day_value(day: Day): Int {
    Day(n) = day
    n
}

fn days_value(days: Days): Int {
    Days(n) = days
    n
}

impl Add<Days, Day> for Day {
    fn add(lhs: Day, rhs: Days): Day {
        Day(day_value(lhs) + days_value(rhs))
    }
}

// Both arguments are BOUND rather than written as constructor calls, and that
// is a measured avoidance rather than tidiness: `Days(step())` makes the
// interpreter record the inner `step() = 2` while the builder's constructor
// path does not, which is a SEPARATE gap on the constructor argument recording
// and not this one. Bundling it here would attribute two gaps to one fix.
test "the operator interface written as a call records its arguments" {
    ten = Day(10)
    four = Days(4)
    assert day_value(Add.add(ten, four)) == 99
}

test "the operator impl named by its type records its arguments" {
    ten = Day(10)
    four = Days(4)
    assert day_value(Day.add(ten, four)) == 99
}
